# Cursor Rules for Info Site Project

## Project Overview

This is a Next.js 15+ project using App Router with TypeScript, TailwindCSS, and modern React patterns. The project follows a clean, organized structure for building an information website.

## Project Structure

```
project-root/
├── public/                     # Static files (images, favicon, robots.txt, etc.)
│   ├── images/                # Static images
│   └── icons/                 # SVG or PNG icons
├── src/
│   ├── app/                   # Next.js App Router (Next 13+)
│   ├── components/            # Reusable UI components
│   │   ├── common/            # Shared components (Header, Footer)
│   │   │   ├── header/        # Header component folder
│   │   │   │   ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │   │   │   └── header.module.scss # Component styles
│   │   │   └── footer/        # Footer component folder
│   │   │       ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │   │       └── footer.module.scss # Component styles
│   │   ├── sections/          # Page sections (Hero, About, Services, etc.)
│   │   │   ├── hero-section/  # Hero section folder
│   │   │   │   ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │   │   │   └── hero-section.module.scss # Component styles
│   │   │   └── contact-section/ # Contact section folder
│   │   │       ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │   │       └── contact-section.module.scss # Component styles
│   │   └── ui/                # Atomic UI components (Button, Input, Card, etc.)
│   │       ├── button/        # Button component folder
│   │       │   ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │       │   └── button.module.scss # Component styles
│   │       ├── card/          # Card component folder
│   │       │   ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │   │   └── card.module.scss # Component styles
│   │       └── input/         # Input component folder
│   │           ├── index.tsx  # Main component file
│   │           └── input.module.scss # Component styles
│   ├── contexts/              # React Context (global state)
│   ├── hooks/                 # Custom hooks
│   ├── types/                 # TypeScript types/interfaces
│   ├── utils/                 # Utility functions (helpers)
│   └── styles/                # SCSS organization and global styles
│       ├── tailwind.css       # TailwindCSS directives
│       ├── index.scss         # Main entry point - imports all styles
│       ├── globals.scss       # Global styles (CSS variables, base styles)
│       ├── layout.scss        # Layout utilities (spacing, containers, grids)
│       ├── typography.scss    # Typography classes (headings, text styles)
│       ├── utilities.scss     # Utility classes (icons, responsive, animations)
│       ├── animations.scss    # Custom animations and keyframes
├── tailwind.config.ts         # TailwindCSS configuration
├── postcss.config.js          # PostCSS configuration
├── tsconfig.json              # TypeScript configuration
├── package.json
└── next.config.js             # Next.js configuration
```

## Technology Stack

-   **Framework**: Next.js 15+ with App Router
-   **Language**: TypeScript
-   **Styling**: TailwindCSS + SCSS (modular architecture)
-   **State Management**: React Context + Custom Hooks
-   **Build Tool**: Vite (via Next.js)

## Coding Standards

### TypeScript

-   Use strict TypeScript configuration
-   Define proper interfaces and types in `src/types/`
-   Avoid `any` type - use proper typing
-   Use type inference when possible
-   Export types from `src/types/index.d.ts`

### React Components

-   Use functional components with hooks
-   Follow React 18+ best practices
-   Use proper prop typing with interfaces
-   Implement proper error boundaries
-   Use React.memo() for performance optimization when needed

### File Naming Conventions

-   **Components**: kebab-case (e.g., `hero-section.tsx`, `contact-form.tsx`)
-   **Hooks**: camelCase with `use` prefix (e.g., `useTheme.ts`, `useWindowSize.ts`)
-   **Utils**: camelCase (e.g., `formatDate.ts`, `validateEmail.ts`)
-   **Types**: camelCase (e.g., `userTypes.ts`, `apiTypes.ts`)
-   **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `API_ENDPOINTS.ts`)
-   **SCSS Files**: kebab-case (e.g., `hero-section.scss`, `button.scss`)

### Component Structure

```typescript
// src/components/sections/hero-section/index.tsx
import React from "react";
import { HeroSectionProps } from "@/types";
import styles from "./hero-section.module.scss"; // Import component styles

export const HeroSection: React.FC<HeroSectionProps> = ({ title, description }) => {
    return <section className={styles.heroSection}>{/* Component content */}</section>;
};
```

### Import/Export Patterns

-   Use absolute imports with `@/` alias (configured in tsconfig.json)
-   Group imports: React, external libraries, internal modules, types
-   Use named exports for components and functions
-   Use default exports only for pages and layouts
-   Import SCSS modules in components: `import styles from './component-name.module.scss'`
-   Use CSS modules for scoped styling: `className={styles.componentClass}`

### Styling Guidelines

-   **SCSS Architecture**: Use modular SCSS where each component has its own folder
-   **Component Structure**: Each component folder contains `index.tsx` and `component-name.module.scss`
-   **CSS Modules**: Use CSS modules for scoped styling to prevent style conflicts
-   **Global Styles**: Keep `globals.scss` minimal - only CSS variables, base styles, scrollbar, print styles
-   **Component Styles**: Each component should import its own SCSS module
-   **Utility Styles**: Organize common styles into logical files (layout, typography, utilities, animations)
-   **TailwindCSS**: Use utility classes as primary styling method with `@apply` for custom components
-   **CSS Variables**: Use CSS custom properties for theme values
-   **Responsive Design**: Follow mobile-first approach with Tailwind responsive prefixes

### SCSS Organization Rules

#### File Structure

```
src/styles/
├── index.scss              # Main entry point - imports all styles
├── globals.scss            # Global styles only (variables, base, scrollbar)
├── layout.scss             # Layout utilities (spacing, containers, grids)
├── typography.scss         # Typography classes (headings, text styles)
├── utilities.scss          # Utility classes (icons, responsive, animations)
├── animations.scss         # Custom animations and keyframes
└── components/             # Component-specific styles
    ├── sections/           # Section component styles
    │   ├── hero-section.module.scss
    │   └── contact-section.module.scss
    ├── ui/                 # UI component styles
    │   ├── button.module.scss
    │   ├── card.module.scss
    │   └── input.module.scss
    └── common/             # Common component styles
        ├── header.module.scss
        └── footer.module.scss
```

#### Component SCSS Rules

-   Create component folder with same name as component
-   Place `index.tsx` (main component) and `component-name.module.scss` in component folder
-   Use descriptive class names that match component
-   Import SCSS module in component: `import styles from './component-name.module.scss'`
-   Use CSS modules for scoped styling: `className={styles.componentClass}`
-   Add SCSS import to `src/styles/index.scss` for global availability

#### Utility SCSS Rules

-   Use `layout.scss` for spacing, containers, grid layouts
-   Use `typography.scss` for text styles, headings, gradients
-   Use `utilities.scss` for icons, responsive helpers, focus styles
-   Use `animations.scss` for keyframes, animation classes, hover effects

### State Management

-   Use React Context for global state (theme, user preferences)
-   Use local state with useState for component-specific state
-   Use useReducer for complex state logic
-   Implement proper state persistence when needed

### Performance Best Practices

-   Use Next.js Image component for optimized images
-   Implement proper lazy loading for components
-   Use React.memo() and useMemo() strategically
-   Optimize bundle size with dynamic imports
-   Use proper caching strategies
-   Use CSS modules for scoped styling to prevent style conflicts
-   Import SCSS modules only where needed to avoid unused CSS

### Error Handling

-   Implement proper error boundaries
-   Use try-catch blocks in async operations
-   Provide meaningful error messages to users
-   Log errors appropriately for debugging

### Testing Considerations

-   Write testable code with proper separation of concerns
-   Use descriptive component and function names
-   Implement proper prop validation
-   Keep components pure and predictable

### Accessibility

-   Use semantic HTML elements
-   Implement proper ARIA labels
-   Ensure keyboard navigation support
-   Maintain proper color contrast ratios
-   Use proper heading hierarchy

### SEO Optimization

-   Use Next.js built-in SEO features
-   Implement proper meta tags
-   Use semantic HTML structure
-   Optimize for Core Web Vitals
-   Implement proper sitemap and robots.txt

## File Organization Rules

### Components Directory

-   `common/`: Shared components used across multiple pages
-   Each component has its own folder (e.g., `header/`, `footer/`)
-   Each folder contains `index.tsx` and `component-name.module.scss`
-   `sections/`: Page-specific sections (Hero, Features, Contact, etc.)
-   Each section has its own folder (e.g., `hero-section/`, `contact-section/`)
-   Each folder contains `index.tsx` and `component-name.module.scss`
-   `ui/`: Atomic UI components (Button, Input, Modal, etc.)
-   Each component has its own folder (e.g., `button/`, `card/`, `input/`)
-   Each folder contains `index.tsx` and `component-name.module.scss`

### Styles Directory

-   `index.scss`: Main entry point that imports all styles
-   `globals.scss`: Only truly global styles (variables, base, scrollbar, print)
-   `layout.scss`: Layout utilities and grid systems
-   `typography.scss`: Text styles and typography classes
-   `utilities.scss`: Helper classes and utility functions
-   `animations.scss`: Custom animations and keyframes
-   `components/`: Component-specific styles organized by component type

### Hooks Directory

-   Keep hooks focused and single-purpose
-   Use descriptive names that indicate their purpose
-   Implement proper cleanup in useEffect hooks
-   Return proper types from custom hooks

### Utils Directory

-   Keep utility functions pure and testable
-   Use proper error handling
-   Implement proper input validation
-   Export functions individually for tree-shaking

### Types Directory

-   Group related types in separate files
-   Use descriptive interface names
-   Implement proper generic types when needed
-   Export all types from index.d.ts

## Development Workflow

1. Create new component folder in appropriate directories
2. Create `index.tsx` (main component) and `component-name.module.scss` in component folder
3. Import SCSS module in component: `import styles from './component-name.module.scss'`
4. Use CSS modules for styling: `className={styles.componentClass}`
5. Add SCSS import to `src/styles/index.scss`
6. Define types before implementing components
7. Use TailwindCSS classes for styling
8. Implement proper error handling
9. Test components in different screen sizes
10. Ensure accessibility compliance
11. Optimize for performance

## Common Patterns

### Theme Context

```typescript
// src/contexts/theme-context.tsx
export const ThemeContext = createContext<ThemeContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const ThemeProvider: React.FC<{ children: React.ReactNode }> = ({ children }) => {
    // Theme logic
};
```

### Custom Hooks

```typescript
// src/hooks/useTheme.ts
export const useTheme = () => {
    const context = useContext(ThemeContext);
    if (!context) {
        throw new Error("useTheme must be used within ThemeProvider");
    }
    return context;
};
```

### Component with SCSS

```typescript
// src/components/ui/button/index.tsx
import React from "react";
import { ButtonProps } from "@/types";
import styles from "./button.module.scss"; // Import component styles

export const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ children, className }) => (
    <button className={`${styles.btnPrimary} ${className}`}>{children}</button>
);
```

```scss
// src/components/ui/button/button.module.scss
.btnPrimary {
    @apply bg-blue-600 text-white font-semibold py-2 px-4 rounded-lg transition-colors duration-300;

    &:hover {
        @apply bg-blue-700;
    }
}
```

### API Utilities

```typescript
// src/utils/api.ts
export const apiClient = {
    get: async <T>(url: string): Promise<T> => {
        // API logic
    },
};
```

## Dependencies to Avoid

-   Avoid jQuery or other DOM manipulation libraries
-   Avoid CSS-in-JS solutions (use TailwindCSS + SCSS modules)
-   Avoid heavy animation libraries unless necessary
-   Avoid unnecessary state management libraries

## Performance Targets

-   First Contentful Paint: < 1.5s
-   Largest Contentful Paint: < 2.5s
-   Cumulative Layout Shift: < 0.1
-   First Input Delay: < 100ms

## Browser Support

-   Modern browsers (Chrome 90+, Firefox 88+, Safari 14+)
-   Mobile browsers (iOS Safari 14+, Chrome Mobile 90+)
-   Progressive enhancement for older browsers

## Build and Deployment

-   Use Next.js built-in optimization features
-   Implement proper environment variable handling
-   Use proper build caching strategies
-   Optimize for Vercel deployment

Remember: Keep code clean, readable, and maintainable. Follow React and TypeScript best practices, use modular SCSS architecture with CSS modules, and always consider performance and accessibility in your implementations.
